// `add_param` writes one of the two statements a parameter is: the port node the body computes
// with. The signature is the other one, and the one statement that can write it today is a C++
// pass -- no block head exists (design graph-editing 8.4). `one_parameter.cml` reads both halves
// at the commit; what neither case did was *run* the graph they publish, and an operator whose
// graph is published but never executed is one step from an operator that does not exist.
//
// This is the execution. `test::add_param` widens the graph `main` calls: a third parameter of
// type `int`, the signature rewritten to declare it, and the body's reads of its second parameter
// handed to the new port. It widens the call site too, because a parameter's value has exactly one
// place to come from at run time -- the caller's argument list, bound to the port's slot when the
// call frame is laid out. The command line cannot stand in for that caller: `--arg` binds the
// entry's parameters *before* the first pass and substitutes a constant for each port it binds
// (`entry_args.cpp#bindEntryArguments`), so a parameter a pass adds to the entry can never be
// given a value.
//
// The reading is one number. `pair(41, 1)` is 4101; the same program over the widened graph prints
// the value the caller passed for the parameter that was added. The port is *appended* -- its slot
// is the last one the graph lays out (`addParameterPort`) -- so what the run says is whether the
// value a caller passes for a new parameter lands in the place that parameter names.
//
// `pair` is named because the pass has to find it: it is the graph `main` calls, and it is the one
// this case is about.

func pair(a: int, b: int) => int {
    return a * 100 + b
}

func main() => int sync {
    println(`[add_param_run] pair=${pair(41, 1)}`)
    return 0
}